{
  "dom_id": "step-q101832",
  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "If $1-3fg=2f-1,$ then $f=$",
  "worked_solution": "We need to isolate all terms with $f$ on one side of the equation. To do this, we start by subtracting $1$ from both sides: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-3fg & =2f-1 \\\\ 1-3fg-1 & =2f-1-1 \\\\ -3fg & =2f-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we subtract $2f$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3fg & =2f-2 \\\\ -3fg-2f & =2f-2-2f \\\\ -3fg-2f & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that all terms containing $f$ are on the left-hand side, and all terms independent of $f$ are on the right-hand side.\n\nNext, we factor out $f$ in the expression on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3fg-2f & =-2 \\\\ f(-3g-2) & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide both sides by $(-3g-2)$ to isolate $f:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-3g-2) & =-2 \\\\ \\frac{f(-3g-2)}{-3g-2} & =\\frac{-2}{-3g-2} \\\\ \\frac{f\\cancel{(-3g-2)}}{\\cancel{-3g-2}} & =\\frac{-2}{-(3g+2)} \\\\ f & =\\frac{2}{3g+2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** This result assumes that $3g+2\\ne 0.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
